// expected: cannot find package
//
// An import whose path resolves to no package is an ERROR, in every position.
//
// Until round 103 this compiler skipped such an import when the importer was a
// dependency (only the package the build was ASKED for was reported), so a
// blank import of a package that does not exist compiled to nothing and the
// build returned 0. legacy has always answered `package %q not found`
// (loader/mxlist.go resolveDir), so this is the missing half of a rule both
// compilers must share.
//
// What it hid, measured: four entries of the phase-1b stdlib sweep -
// encoding/json, encoding/xml, regexp and text/template - name packages that
// do not exist in this tree at all (encoding/json and encoding/xml went with
// the reflect/any exodus in 3b6e25e8 and 4da18fb0; regexp and text/template
// with the same sweep), and `_ "encoding/json"` compiled to nothing while the
// sweep reported "41 packages ok". Ten more files (internal/lazyregexp,
// internal/lazytemplate, internal/coverage/{cfile,pods},
// internal/trace/{testtrace,traceviewer}, internal/profile/prune.mx,
// index/suffixarray, and the stale _mxc_stage4/{syntax,token,cmd/mxlex}
// copies) imported packages that do not exist and were never built by
// anything - they are removed in the same round.
//
// The blank import is the case that must not regress: a used import fails
// later anyway, but a blank one has no symbol to miss, and a side-effect
// import that silently does not happen is the worst version of this bug.
package main

import (
	_ "nonexistent/pkg"
)

func main() {
	println("unreachable")
}
